(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-hypotension-vignettes"]={"title":"Hypotension Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 78-year-old man fell in the bathroom at 3 a.m. while urinating standing up, after two beers the evening before. He felt a swimmy head, nausea and sweating first and recovered quickly. His vital signs, neurologic examination and glucose are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Situational reflex syncope","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Hypoglycemic loss of consciousness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nocturnal voiding after abruptly rising triggers an exaggerated autonomic reflex, and alcohol and relative dehydration add to it; this is micturition syncope. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman fainted once after standing for an hour in the heat at a graduation, with warmth and nausea first and rapid recovery. Her examination and readings are normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Begin midodrine daily","Advise avoiding the trigger","Start fludrocortisone","Refer for echocardiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vasovagal syncope is treated by trigger avoidance, here prolonged standing in the heat, with safety precautions when unavoidable. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in a clinic has hypotension from a suspected overwhelming infection and is awaiting transfer. Which therapy should not be started in the clinic?","choices":["Arranging urgent transfer","Repeating her vital signs","Reviewing her medications","A norepinephrine infusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension asks whether she can keep a vasopressor at home for bad days. What should she be told?","choices":["Pressors are hospital-only drugs","A home supply is reasonable","Only if she avoids salt","Only after a tilt-table test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has had diarrhea and poor oral intake for 3 days and fainted when he stood up quickly this morning. His oral mucous membranes are dry. Supine he is 134/76 with a heart rate of 66; standing he is 104/62 with a heart rate of 94. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Situational reflex syncope","Postural orthostatic tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A 30 mmHg systolic fall with a heart rate rise from 66 to 94 shows an intact sympathetic reflex, and diarrhea with poor intake explains the volume depletion. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man lost consciousness at work. Coworkers saw rhythmic jerking of all four limbs, he bit his tongue and was incontinent, and he remained confused for 30 minutes. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["A seizure","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Situational reflex syncope","Orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tongue biting, incontinence and prolonged confusion afterward point to seizure, a non-hypotensive cause; syncope ends in rapid, spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man reports lightheadedness and dimming vision within a minute or two of standing, which settles when he sits. His examination at rest is normal. Which test should be performed first?","choices":["A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram","A stress test","Orthostatic vital signs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pressure and heart rate taken supine and again within 3 minutes of standing show whether orthostatic criteria are met and which type the heart rate response suggests. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with a known cardiomyopathy has had two faints on exertion. Her electrocardiogram shows no arrhythmia. Which test best evaluates a structural cause?","choices":["A blood glucose level","An episode log","An echocardiogram","A tilt-table test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Structural causes, infarction, cardiomyopathy, heart failure and valve disease, are evaluated with echocardiography or stress echocardiography plus cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with coronary disease felt her heart race and then collapsed without warning in a store. She has recovered and is stable in the office. Which test should be obtained first?","choices":["An electrocardiogram","A tilt-table test","An episode log","Orthostatic vital signs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A suspected arrhythmia is investigated first with an electrocardiogram, then monitoring, a stress test and cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with Parkinson disease reports lightheadedness and dimming vision within minutes of standing and fainted once in his kitchen. He is well hydrated. Supine he is 138/80 with a heart rate of 68; standing he is 106/70 with a heart rate of 70. What is the most likely cause of his syncope?","choices":["Autonomic failure from Parkinson disease","Volume depletion from poor intake","A vasovagal reflex on standing","A ventricular arrhythmia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Parkinson disease is an example cause of neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, and a large pressure fall with almost no heart rate rise marks autonomic failure. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction heart failure has run low pressures for a year and asks whether his low pressure will simply go away. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["It always resolves within weeks","It needs a vasopressor at home","Its cause may not be removable","It will end once a trigger is found"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic hypotension waxes and wanes, and its underlying cause, here heart failure, may be unavoidable, unlike an acute trigger that can be removed. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has diabetic autonomic neuropathy and also congestive heart failure. He faints on standing. Which of the following should be avoided?","choices":["Treating the underlying cause","A careful history","Fludrocortisone","Slow position changes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fludrocortisone causes salt and water retention and is avoided in congestive heart failure, even though it is best with the tachycardia syndrome. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man takes an alpha-1 blocker and lisinopril and has fainted twice while urinating at night after rising abruptly from bed. His examination and electrocardiogram are normal. Which is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Admit for a vasopressor infusion","Order a tilt-table test urgently","Reduce contributing medications","Start midodrine for reflex syncope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An alpha-1 blocker with an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor predisposes to micturition syncope, and iatrogenic contributors are reduced or removed. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with diabetic autonomic neuropathy takes midodrine but still has lightheadedness on standing. He has no supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat). Which addition is most appropriate?","choices":["Phenylephrine at home","Strict fluid restriction","Vasopressin in clinic","Droxidopa as an adjunct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Droxidopa has mild efficacy and is usually used as an adjunct with midodrine or fludrocortisone rather than on its own. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with Parkinson disease has neurogenic orthostatic hypotension and supine hypertension (high blood pressure while lying flat). Which medication is best suited to her?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Vasopressin","Droxidopa","Midodrine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Droxidopa has mild efficacy but is better with supine hypertension, the setting in which midodrine is avoided. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with 20 years of type 2 diabetes has fainted twice shortly after getting out of bed. Her mucous membranes are moist, and vibration and light-touch sensation are reduced in both feet. Supine she is 140/80 with a heart rate of 70; after 3 minutes standing she is 110/66 with a heart rate of 74. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Cardiogenic arrhythmic syncope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The systolic fall of 30 mmHg meets the criterion, the heart rate barely rises, and long-standing diabetes with neuropathy points to diabetic autonomic neuropathy. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with diabetic autonomic neuropathy also has congestive heart failure. His family asks what could raise his standing pressure. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":["Slow position changes","Treating the cause","Fludrocortisone","Droxidopa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fludrocortisone causes salt and water retention and is avoided in congestive heart failure, whatever its benefit in the tachycardia syndrome. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has a faint while urinating at night, with nausea and sweating beforehand and quick recovery. Which test is used to exclude a cardiac dysrhythmia?","choices":["Orthostatic vital signs","An electrocardiogram","A tilt-table test","A blood glucose level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A normal electrocardiogram lowers suspicion of a dysrhythmia, just as a normal glucose excludes hypoglycemia and blood tests address infection. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome remains symptomatic despite increasing her water and salt intake. She has no heart failure. Which medication is most appropriate?","choices":["Norepinephrine","Fludrocortisone","Midodrine","Droxidopa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fludrocortisone is best with the syndrome; because it causes salt and water retention it is avoided only in heart failure, which she does not have. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy has lightheadedness on standing. Within 10 minutes his heart rate rises from 68 to 102, and his pressure is unchanged. Does this meet the heart rate criterion for his age?","choices":["No; his rate must exceed 150","No; adolescents need a rise of 40","Yes; the rise exceeds 30 per minute","Yes; any rise with symptoms counts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His rise of 34 would exceed the adult threshold of 30, but in adolescents the threshold is an increase of 40 beats per minute. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is recovering from a stomach virus that left him eating and drinking little. He fainted on standing, and orthostatic readings show a large pressure fall with a brisk heart rate rise. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Start a daily midodrine tablet","Restrict salt until he recovers","Expect the faints to be permanent","Keep up fluids while intake is poor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Poor intake and dehydration are listed causes of non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, and treating the underlying cause, here the volume loss, is the aim. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has recurrent faints with a prodrome, but after a careful history the source is still uncertain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin midodrine empirically","Refer to the right specialty","Start a vasopressor at home","Tell her the faints are harmless"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of diagnosing reflex syncope, or of referring to the right specialty for confirmation when it stays uncertain. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with orthostatic hypotension keeps standing up quickly despite being told not to, because he does not see why it matters. Which approach most improves his adherence?","choices":["Explain why the pressure falls","Add a vasopressor at home","Repeat the same instruction","Stop discussing it with him"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Understanding is the key to compliance: once a patient knows why the pressure falls on standing, slow position changes make sense and are followed. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has vomited for 2 days, has dry mucous membranes, and fainted on standing; her heart rate rises from 70 to 98 as her pressure falls 30 mmHg. Which is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Start midodrine for autonomic failure","Order a tilt-table test first","Refer her for an echocardiogram","Treat the underlying volume loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her intact heart rate response and dryness point to volume depletion, and orthostasis is managed by treating the underlying condition. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man had a large blood loss after surgery 2 days ago and now feels faint on standing. His pressure falls 30 mmHg with a heart rate rise from 72 to 100. Which is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Order a tilt-table test","Treat the underlying blood loss","Start midodrine for neurogenic syncope","Start fludrocortisone for fluid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemorrhage is an example cause of non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, and the principle is to treat the underlying condition, the real diagnosis. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes fainted after walking to the bathroom. Supine she is 146/84 with a heart rate of 70; after 3 minutes standing she is 118/70 with a heart rate of 72. What does her heart rate response indicate?","choices":["Volume depletion is the cause","An arrhythmia is present","The autonomic response has failed","The autonomic response is intact"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pressure fall with only a minimal heart rate rise means the sympathetic reflex is not compensating, which favors neurogenic orthostatic hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has his pressure recorded at a well visit, and a new staff member flags it as low by comparing it with an adult's normal range. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Measure it again in adult units","Compare it with norms for his age","Start a vasopressor for hypotension","Order an echocardiogram at once"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blood pressure norms are affected by age, so a child's reading is judged against the normal range for his age rather than an adult's. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has fainted twice, each time with warmth and nausea beforehand and quick recovery. Which part of his evaluation is most useful in establishing the diagnosis?","choices":["A stress test","The history","A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of diagnosing reflex syncope, summed up as history, history, history, supported by a log of episodes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man's electrocardiogram shows runs of nonsustained ventricular tachycardia after an abrupt faint preceded by palpitations. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Neurology consultation","Endocrinology referral","Physical therapy referral","Cardiology consultation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every type of cardiogenic syncope, arrhythmic, structural and vascular, lists cardiology consultation alongside its tests. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had his diuretic dose increased 10 days ago. He now has lightheadedness on standing, a 28 mmHg systolic fall and a heart rate rise from 70 to 94. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Start midodrine for the pressure","Start fludrocortisone","Add droxidopa as an adjunct","Reduce the recently increased diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The offending agent is removed first: iatrogenic hypotension is treated by reducing or removing the medication, with slow position changes meanwhile. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
